Subject: [PATCH 2/3] Detect MIDI ports lost to a USB drop and reopen them by name

A controller that drops off the USB bus (unplugged, USB reset, power dip)
raises no error : its WinMM handle just goes silent, for good. Windows lists
the device again once it is back, but as a new device that only a new open
reaches. MidiHub never noticed :

 - IsConnected was `_ports.Count > 0` : a count of managed objects, never
   checked against what Windows lists, so the status stayed green over a dead
   port ;
 - PollNewPorts returned as soon as `portCount <= _ports.Count`. A controller
   coming back leaves the count unchanged, so it was never reopened, and only
   restarting the editor brought it back ;
 - PollNewPorts also assumed new ports appear at the end of the list
   (`for i = _ports.Count ..`). Windows renumbers on every arrival : a device
   plugged in at a lower index made it reopen a device already open, under its
   new suffixed name, and never open the new one ;
 - an open that RtMidi refused (reported only through the ok flag) still went
   into _ports, shown as connected.

In this project the controllers dropped off the bus 17 times in ten days
(Kernel-PnP event 1010), and every RtMidi crash in the Application log came
after one of them : silent port, then a Reconnect / play-mode reload that
closed it (see patch 1).

TryOpenPorts and PollNewPorts are replaced by a single watchdog on
EditorApplication.update, paced on real time (the old 3 s timer counted
editor ticks). Twice a second it lists the inputs Windows offers and :

 - closes, through the quarantine-aware SafeDispose, every open port that is
   no longer listed, and reports it lost (warning in the console,
   MidiHub.LostPortNames, a HelpBox in the MIDI Map tab) ;
 - opens every listed port that is not open : first open, after a Reconnect,
   or a lost one coming back. A refused open is not kept ; it is logged once
   and retried every 2 s.

Ports are keyed by name without the " <index>" suffix RtMidi appends on WinMM,
so a renumbered device is recognised ; a second identical device gets " #2".
Each In is kept with the Out matched to it, and both are closed together.
The probe interval goes to 2 s while no input is listed at all, because RtMidi
prints a warning on every probe in that state.

Reconnect now only closes everything and clears the lost list ; the watchdog
reopens on the next tick.

KontrolsWindow resends its feedback when the number of open ports grows, so
motorised knobs match the window after a comeback -- and after a domain
reload, where the SendAllFeedback in OnEnable runs before any port is open.
It does so at most once per 5 s : if moving every motor at once is what knocks
a bus-powered controller off USB, an unthrottled resync would loop.

New public API : MidiHub.LostPortNames, MidiHub.HasOutput. PortNames now holds
the keys (e.g. "Roto-Control" instead of "Roto-Control 0").

Known limit : a drop shorter than the 0.5 s period (gone and back between two
probes) is not seen ; Reconnect, now safe, remains the manual fallback.

Depends on patch 1 : without it, closing a lost port crashes the editor.
